OCGRAPH 1
DOMAIN gameplay
# A SELF-SEEDING IDLE MOTION, and the point of it is that it belongs to no project.
#
# One .ocgraph file, driving any number of entities, each animating around ITS OWN starting point.
# Nothing about a game, a genre or a level appears below: it reads whatever entity it is handed,
# remembers where that entity was when play began, and moves it in a small orbit around there.
# Drop it on a sign, a lantern, a fish, a shooting-range target -- it does not know or care.
#
# WHAT THIS REPLACES, AND WHY IT IS A DESIGN CHANGE RATHER THAN A PORT. The shape this pattern grew
# out of could only ever emit an OFFSET: the graph had no way to learn its own entity's position, so
# the host had to add the entity's spawn point back on afterwards. That meant every project driving
# several entities from one shared graph had to supply a bespoke per-entity position sink in C#, and
# any project that forgot got every entity stacked on the world origin. Two engine additions remove
# the need for that entirely:
#   GetFieldVec3 (75be56b) lets a graph READ its own transform, and
#   VAR/SetVar/GetVar (a45e32f) lets it REMEMBER what it read.
# So the graph writes absolute positions itself, and needs no host code at all. It needs no OUT
# records either, for its own logic -- the three at the very bottom of this file exist ONLY so the
# tick log prints the numbers it wrote, and deleting them changes nothing about what moves. See their
# own comment down there before assuming a graph like this owes anyone an output.
#
# WHY OnStart AND NOT A first-tick BRANCH. GraphHost.Tick fires OnStart once, in the same tick, and
# strictly BEFORE OnTick (GraphHost.cs, the _startInvoked block) -- and both entry points share one
# variable pool, because storage lives on the host rather than per entry point. So the seed lands
# before the first read of it, with no "have I initialised yet" flag and no one-frame glitch where
# the entity snaps to the origin. There is no NOT node in the vocabulary today either, which would
# have made the branch form awkward as well as unnecessary.
#
# WHAT IT CAPTURES, stated because it is a semantic and not an accident: home is where the entity
# sat when PLAY BEGAN, not where it was spawned and not where it is now. Move the entity by other
# means afterwards and it will keep orbiting the old spot. That is the same behaviour a spawn-time
# position sink had, so nothing regressed -- but it is a choice, and a graph that wanted to follow
# a moving anchor would re-seed rather than seed once.
NAME IdleMotion
DESCRIPTION Remembers its entity's position at play start, then orbits it. Self-contained: any entity, no host code, no position sink.

# CLASS <name> [parent] -- OPTIONAL, and additive: everything above and below this line is
# unchanged from the file's original synthetic-entity-discovery shape. Declaring this turns the
# SAME graph into a real, spawnable actor class (AN_ prefix, per this engine's Aver Node class
# convention) -- a level can PLACE ... class AN_Orbiter and get a real entity whose own
# OnStart/OnTick this file drives, with its own independent VAR storage (see the a45e32f note
# above on GraphVarStore) instead of the synthetic negative id GameApp's own project-graph
# discovery uses. See HostBridge.cs's "graph classes" region for exactly how a CLASS record is
# registered and bound.
CLASS AN_Orbiter Actor

# entity and time are two of the three names an event-driven GraphHost will supply (the third is
# deltaTime). A VAR is the opposite of a PARAM: the caller owns a PARAM, the graph owns a VAR.
PARAM entity int
PARAM time float

VAR homeX float 0
VAR homeY float 0
VAR homeZ float 0

# ---- tunables. Amplitudes in centimetres, speeds in radians/second. ----------------------------
NODE bob_amp ConstFloat
PIN bob_amp value out float 9.0
NODE bob_speed ConstFloat
PIN bob_speed value out float 1.7
NODE drift_amp ConstFloat
PIN drift_amp value out float 5.0
NODE drift_speed ConstFloat
PIN drift_speed value out float 0.72
# 0.72 / 1.7 is deliberately not a simple ratio, so the vertical and horizontal motions are never
# both at a turning point on the same tick and the combined path does not visibly repeat.

NODE t Param param=time
NODE ent Param param=entity

# ---- seed: run once, before the first OnTick ----------------------------------------------------
ENTRY seed OnStart
NODE seed OnStart

NODE here GetFieldVec3 field=CLocal.position
LINK ent.value here.entity

# Chained through each SetVar's own `then` rather than through Sequence, which has only two exec
# outputs -- three writes would otherwise need two nested Sequence nodes to say the same thing.
NODE setHomeX SetVar var=homeX
NODE setHomeY SetVar var=homeY
NODE setHomeZ SetVar var=homeZ
LINK seed.exec setHomeX.exec
LINK here.x setHomeX.value
LINK setHomeX.then setHomeY.exec
LINK here.y setHomeY.value
LINK setHomeY.then setHomeZ.exec
LINK here.z setHomeZ.value

# ---- tick: orbit the remembered point ------------------------------------------------------------
ENTRY tick OnTick
NODE tick OnTick

NODE bob_phase Multiply
LINK t.value bob_phase.a
LINK bob_speed.value bob_phase.b
NODE bob_sin Sin
LINK bob_phase.result bob_sin.a
NODE bob Multiply
LINK bob_amp.value bob.a
LINK bob_sin.result bob.b

NODE drift_phase Multiply
LINK t.value drift_phase.a
LINK drift_speed.value drift_phase.b
NODE drift_cos Cos
LINK drift_phase.result drift_cos.a
NODE drift Multiply
LINK drift_amp.value drift.a
LINK drift_cos.result drift.b

NODE homeX GetVar var=homeX
NODE homeY GetVar var=homeY
NODE homeZ GetVar var=homeZ

NODE outX Add
LINK homeX.value outX.a
LINK drift.result outX.b
NODE outZ Add
LINK homeZ.value outZ.a
LINK bob.result outZ.b

# No drift toward or away from anything: Y stays exactly where it was seeded. A graph that wanted a
# full 3D wander would add a third phase here; two axes is enough to read as alive.
NODE place SetFieldVec3 field=CLocal.position
# EVERY pin spelled out, including the ones the parser would have supplied. SetFieldVec3's default
# shape has NO exec pins -- like SetField, it is reachable as a pure data write -- and the moment a
# node carries any explicit PIN record the parser stops adding defaults to it at all. So declaring
# exec here means declaring the other five too, or they vanish.
PIN place exec in exec
PIN place entity in int
PIN place x in float
PIN place y in float
PIN place z in float
PIN place then out exec
PIN place success out bool
LINK tick.exec place.exec
LINK ent.value place.entity
LINK outX.result place.x
LINK homeY.value place.y
LINK outZ.result place.z

# THREE OUT RECORDS, NAMING THE SAME EXPRESSIONS `place` ALREADY WRITES -- added for GRAPH-AS-CLASS's
# own acceptance evidence, not required by the graph's own logic (this graph had already written its
# position itself via `place`, and still does; OUT is a SEPARATE, free readback of the identical
# numbers, not a second decision about what to write). CompileEntryPoint pulls these as a pure DATA
# expression at the end of whichever entry it compiles -- OnStart included, since Outputs is a
# graph-wide list, not per-entry -- so OnStart's own compiled delegate also returns one (harmless,
# idempotent: OnStart and OnTick run in the same Tick() call, in that order, so OnStart's own
# OUT-triggered write is immediately superseded by OnTick's identical-formula write moments later).
# What this buys: GraphHost.ApplyResult sees a 2+-element result and calls the PositionSink itself
# (redundant with `place`'s own write, same numbers), AND GraphHost.LogEntryFired prints
# "OnTick -> [x, y, z]" instead of "OnTick -> (void)" -- turning a log line that only proves the
# graph TICKED into one that proves WHAT IT WROTE, on stdout, for every entity, every frame, with no
# native readback tool needed.
OUT outX result
OUT homeY value
OUT outZ result
